	</dialog>

	<dialog id="destroyDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="destroyForm">
			<h3>Delete permanently</h3>
			<p class="muted">This destroys the keys for <strong id="destroyName"></strong> and removes its folder. Afterward this copy can <strong>never</strong> be opened again — not with your password, not with a recovery key. This cannot be undone.</p>
			<p class="hint">Backups, mirrors, and any Recovery Kit, recovery key, threshold shares, or emergency seal you exported keep their own keys and are unaffected — destroy those too if you want the data truly gone. If this is your only copy, it is gone forever. To keep a copy, cancel and use Back up or Mirror first.</p>
			<p class="hint" id="destroyCloudNote" hidden>This vault keeps its encrypted data in the cloud. Destroying the keys here makes that data impossible to decrypt, but the provider may still hold it and older versions — delete it there as well if you need it removed.</p>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Type the vault name (<span id="destroyExpect"></span>) to confirm</span>
				<input type="text" id="destroyConfirm" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" required>
			</label>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Vault password</span>
				<input type="text" class="mask" id="destroyPassword" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" required>
				<small class="hint">Only the full read-write password can erase a vault, so knowing its name is not enough. Lost the password? The data is already unrecoverable — use Remove from list instead, then delete the folder yourself.</small>
			</label>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Cancel</button>
				<button value="ok" id="destroyGo" class="primary-btn danger" disabled>Delete permanently</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

