	<dialog id="emergencyDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="emergencyForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Emergency access</h3>
			<!-- Not set up yet: enroll a trusted contact -->
			<div id="emergencyEnroll">
				<p class="hint">Let a trusted contact gain <strong>read-only</strong> access to a vault if you stop checking in. They make a keypair and give you only the public key; you seal a vault's read link to it, so only their private key can ever open it. A timer only decides when the sealed access is handed over.</p>
				<div class="tamper-result warn my-2">
					<strong>Know the limits.</strong> Access is read-only, the timer runs on <em>this</em> computer (so it suits incapacitation, not a lost or destroyed machine), and any check-in cancels a pending release.
					<details class="advanced"><summary>More on the limits</summary>Once released, or if a sealed copy leaks, the contact can read that copy — to truly cancel, rotate the vault's keys, not just disarm. A long trip can trip the timer, which is why there is a grace period. This grants data access, not legal authority.</details>
				</div>
				<label class="field"><span>Contact's public key</span><input type="text" id="emContactKey" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="paste the public key your contact gave you"></label>
				<p class="fine">Don't have one? <button type="button" id="emGenKey" class="link-btn">Generate a keypair to give a contact</button></p>
				<div id="emKeyOut" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
				<label class="field"><span>Contact name (optional)</span><input type="text" id="emContactLabel" autocomplete="off" placeholder="e.g. My sister"></label>
				<div class="row-gap">
					<label class="field flex-1"><span>Check in every (days)</span><input type="text" id="emInactive" inputmode="numeric" value="30" data-digits="1"></label>
					<label class="field flex-1"><span>Grace period (days)</span><input type="text" id="emGrace" inputmode="numeric" value="14" data-digits="1"></label>
				</div>
				<div id="emEnrollResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
				<div class="dialog-actions">
					<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Cancel</button>
					<button value="ok" class="primary-btn">Set up</button>
				</div>
			</div>
			<!-- Set up: status + beneficiaries + check in / arm a vault / disarm -->
			<div id="emergencyManage" hidden>
				<div id="emStatus" class="tamper-result mb-2"></div>
				<div class="field"><span>Beneficiaries</span>
					<div id="emContactsList" class="notes-list"></div>
				</div>
				<details class="advanced"><summary>Add another beneficiary</summary>
					<label class="field"><span>Their public key</span><input type="text" id="emAddKey" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="paste the public key they gave you"></label>
					<p class="fine">Don't have one? <button type="button" id="emGenKey2" class="link-btn">Generate a keypair to give them</button></p>
					<div id="emAddKeyOut" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
					<label class="field"><span>Their name</span><input type="text" id="emAddLabel" autocomplete="off" placeholder="e.g. My business partner"></label>
					<div class="dialog-actions dialog-actions-inline"><button type="button" id="emAddBtn" class="ghost-btn">Add beneficiary</button></div>
				</details>
				<label class="field"><span>Route a vault to a beneficiary (seals its read access to them)</span>
					<select id="emArmVault"></select>
				</label>
				<label class="field"><span>Beneficiary</span><select id="emArmContact"></select></label>
				<label class="field"><span>Vault password</span><input type="text" class="mask" id="emArmPassword" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" placeholder="to authorize read-only access"></label>
				<div id="emManageResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
				<div class="dialog-actions wrap-gap">
					<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
					<button type="button" id="emArmBtn" class="primary-btn">Route this vault</button>
					<button type="button" id="emCheckinBtn" class="ghost-btn">Check in</button>
					<button type="button" id="emDisarmBtn" class="link-btn danger">Turn off</button>
				</div>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="travelDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="travelForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Travel mode</h3>
			<div id="travelEnable">
				<p class="hint">Before you travel, hide every vault from this app and lock them all. The app will look empty. A separate travel password restores them afterward.</p>
				<div class="tamper-result warn my-2">
					<strong>Be clear about what this does.</strong> It hides the vaults from <em>this app</em> and locks them, defeating a casual look — but it does <strong>not</strong> remove the encrypted data from your disk, and it cannot defeat a forensic disk image, a backup, or traces the system keeps. In many places you can be legally compelled to disclose passwords.
					<details class="advanced"><summary>More on the limits</summary>Hiding or wiping data during an inspection can itself be an offense. The only strong protection at a border is to <strong>not carry the data</strong> — leave it at home and fetch it over the network afterward. Do this calmly before you travel, never at the checkpoint.</details>
				</div>
				<label class="field">
					<span>Travel password</span>
					<input type="text" class="mask" id="travelPassword" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" placeholder="you will need this to restore your vaults">
				</label>
				<label class="field">
					<span>Confirm travel password</span>
					<input type="text" class="mask" id="travelConfirm" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true">
				</label>
				<div id="travelResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
				<div class="dialog-actions">
					<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Cancel</button>
					<button value="ok" class="primary-btn danger">Hide vaults</button>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div id="travelRestore" hidden>
				<p class="hint">Travel mode is on — your vaults are hidden. Enter the travel password to restore them.</p>
				<label class="field">
					<span>Travel password</span>
					<input type="text" class="mask" id="travelRestorePassword" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" placeholder="the password you set">
				</label>
				<div id="travelRestoreResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
				<div class="dialog-actions">
					<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Cancel</button>
					<button type="button" id="travelRestoreBtn" class="primary-btn">Restore vaults</button>
				</div>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="mobileDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="mobileForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>View files and notes without mounting</h3>
			<p id="mobileName" class="muted"></p>
			<div id="mobileStart">
				<p class="hint">Open this vault's files right here in your browser — each file is decrypted in the page and shown in a built-in viewer, never mounted as a drive and never handed to another app, so no outside app or preview cache ever sees it. Your secure notes open here too, decrypted only in the page. Or get a one-time code to open the vault on a phone instead. Either way access is read-only, and only encrypted data ever leaves this computer.</p>
				<label class="field">
					<span>Vault password</span>
					<input type="text" class="mask" id="mobilePassword" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" placeholder="to authorize read-only access" autofocus>
				</label>
				<div class="dialog-actions">
					<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Cancel</button>
					<button type="button" id="mobileGetCode" class="ghost-btn">Get a code for a phone</button>
					<button value="ok" class="primary-btn">Open in this browser</button>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div id="mobilePaired" hidden>
				<div id="mobileQr" class="mobile-qr"></div>
				<p class="mobile-code" id="mobileCode"></p>
				<p class="hint">Scan the QR with your phone's camera, or open the address below and type the code. The code works once and expires soon.</p>
				<label class="field">
					<span>Address</span>
					<input type="text" id="mobileUrl" data-selectall="1" readonly>
				</label>
				<div id="mobileReach" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
				<div class="dialog-actions">
					<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Done</button>
					<button type="button" id="mobileAnother" class="link-btn">New code</button>
				</div>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="shareDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="shareForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Share with a group</h3>
			<p id="shareName" class="muted"></p>
			<p class="hint">Give a group a password that opens this vault, then hand them the vault itself. Everyone uses the same shared password — separate from your own — and you can change or remove it any time from <strong>Keys</strong> without re‑encrypting anything.</p>
			<details class="advanced"><summary>Sharing with one specific person instead?</summary><p class="hint">Seal read‑only access to their public key from the command line — <code>vdisk share-keypair</code>, then <code>share-seal</code> and <code>share-open</code>. It is post‑quantum, and only their private key can open it.</p></details>
			<fieldset class="share-access">
				<legend>What can they do?</legend>
				<label class="radio-row"><input type="radio" name="shareAccess" value="ro" checked> <span><strong>Read‑only</strong> — open and read the vault, but never change it, add keys, or take snapshots. Safest for sharing.</span></label>
				<label class="radio-row"><input type="radio" name="shareAccess" value="rw"> <span><strong>Read‑write</strong> — open <em>and change</em> the contents. Only for a group you trust to edit; their changes mix with yours.</span></label>
			</fieldset>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Your current password</span>
				<input type="text" class="mask" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" id="shareCurrent">
			</label>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Group password</span>
				<input type="text" class="mask" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" id="sharePassword">
			</label>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Confirm group password</span>
				<input type="text" class="mask" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" id="shareConfirm">
			</label>
			<div id="shareProgress" class="progress" hidden>
				<div class="progress-track"><div id="shareBarFill" class="progress-fill"></div></div>
				<div id="shareBarText" class="progress-text"></div>
			</div>
			<div id="shareResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="share-deliver" id="shareDeliver" hidden>
				<p class="hint">Now get the vault to the group. There are two ways:</p>
				<fieldset class="share-keys">
					<legend>Option 1 — pack a file (recommended): choose which passwords can open it</legend>
					<p class="hint">Only the keys you tick go into the packed file. Your own password is unticked by default, so it will <strong>not</strong> open the copy you hand over — only the group password will.</p>
					<div id="shareKeyList" class="share-key-list"></div>
					<div class="dialog-actions dialog-actions-inline">
						<button type="button" id="sharePack" class="primary-btn">Pack to a file to send…</button>
					</div>
				</fieldset>
				<p class="share-folder-warn"><strong>Option 2 — a shared folder</strong> (a network drive, or a synced folder like Dropbox): each member adds it with <strong>“Add an existing vault folder”</strong> and opens it with the group password. <strong>⚠ A shared folder carries the vault as it is, with every key</strong> — including your own password and any recovery key. To hand over <strong>only</strong> the group password, use the packed file above instead. A read‑write group can work in one shared copy together — one person writes at a time; the others are told when it is in use.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button value="ok" id="shareCreate" class="primary-btn">Create group password</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="backupDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="backupForm">
			<h3>Back up</h3>
			<p id="backupName" class="muted"></p>
			<p class="hint">A vault is already encrypted, so a backup only ever holds scrambled data. Pick a folder on an external drive, a network share, or a synced folder.</p>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Destination</span>
				<select id="backupDest"></select>
			</label>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Automatically</span>
				<select id="backupFreq">
					<option value="off">Off (only when I click Back up now)</option>
					<option value="6">Every 6 hours</option>
					<option value="12">Every 12 hours</option>
					<option value="24">Every 24 hours</option>
					<option value="daily">Daily at a set time</option>
				</select>
			</label>
			<label class="field" id="backupTimeRow" hidden>
				<span>Time (<span id="backupTz">local</span>)</span>
				<input type="time" id="backupTime" value="02:00">
			</label>
			<p class="hint">Automatic backups run while this app is open, and only when the vault is unmounted.</p>
			<div id="backupResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button type="button" id="backupCheck" class="ghost-btn" title="Confirm the backup is a complete, restorable copy of this vault — no password needed">Check backup</button>
				<button type="button" id="backupSave" class="ghost-btn">Save schedule</button>
				<button type="button" id="backupNow" class="primary-btn">Back up now</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="sftpDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="sftpForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Off-site SFTP server</h3>
			<p class="hint">The backup is uploaded already encrypted — the server only ever holds scrambled data. Your login is encrypted where it is saved here, so a copied or synced settings file never exposes it.</p>
			<div class="row">
				<label class="field"><span>Host</span><input type="text" id="sftpHost" placeholder="backup.example.com" autocomplete="off"></label>
				<label class="field"><span>Port</span><input type="text" id="sftpPort" value="22" inputmode="numeric" data-digits="1" maxlength="5" autocomplete="off"></label>
			</div>
			<label class="field"><span>Username</span><input type="text" id="sftpUser" autocomplete="off"></label>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Authenticate with</span>
				<select id="sftpAuth"><option value="password">Password</option><option value="key">SSH key file</option></select>
			</label>
			<label class="field" id="sftpPwRow"><span>Password</span><input type="text" class="mask" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" id="sftpPass" placeholder="leave blank to keep the saved one"></label>
			<label class="field" id="sftpKeyRow" hidden><span>Private key file (path on this computer)</span><input type="text" id="sftpKeyFile" placeholder="~/.ssh/id_ed25519" autocomplete="off"></label>
			<label class="field" id="sftpPassphraseRow" hidden><span>Key passphrase (if any)</span><input type="text" class="mask" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" id="sftpPassphrase" placeholder="leave blank to keep the saved one"></label>
			<label class="field"><span>Remote folder</span><input type="text" id="sftpRemote" placeholder="/backups/vaults" autocomplete="off"></label>
			<label class="field"><span>Label (optional)</span><input type="text" id="sftpLabel" placeholder="Home NAS" autocomplete="off"></label>
				<details class="advanced">
					<summary>Server host key (recommended)</summary>
					<label class="field">
						<span>Host key</span>
						<textarea id="sftpHostKey" rows="3" placeholder="Paste the output of:  ssh-keyscan your-host" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false"></textarea>
						<small class="checkhint">Pins the server's identity so an impersonating server on the network can't capture your login. Get it by running <code>ssh-keyscan &lt;host&gt;</code> on a trusted network, or ask your server's admin, and paste the result here. Leave blank to skip (the connection is then not verified).</small>
						<button type="button" id="sftpUnpin" class="link-btn danger" hidden>Remove the pinned host key</button>
					</label>
				</details>
			<div id="sftpResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Cancel</button>
				<button type="button" id="sftpDelete" class="ghost-btn danger" hidden>Delete</button>
				<button type="button" id="sftpTest" class="ghost-btn">Test</button>
				<button type="button" id="sftpSave" class="primary-btn">Save</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="tamperDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="tamperForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Tamper check</h3>
			<p id="tamperName" class="muted"></p>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Password</span>
				<input type="text" class="mask" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" id="tamperPass" autofocus required>
			</label>
			<p class="hint">Tamper detection is automatic — the vault is checked on every mount and its baseline updates on a clean unmount. This is the optional deep check: "Take snapshot" fingerprints every file's contents and "Audit now" compares against it, catching even a same-size change. Both read every file, so a large vault can take a while.</p>
			<details class="advanced"><summary>Sealing (a strict tripwire)</summary><p class="hint">Seal turns the baseline into a strict tripwire: any file added, removed, or modified is then flagged on every mount and audit, and stays flagged until you accept it by sealing again. Use it for a vault whose contents should not change. "Unseal" returns to automatic tracking.</p></details>
			<details class="advanced"><summary>Timestamp proof</summary><p class="hint">A timestamp proof asks a trusted authority to sign this vault's exact current state together with the time — court-recognized evidence that this state existed by that moment. Only a hash is sent; the vault, its names, and its contents never leave your machine. Proofs are stored beside the vault and travel with your backups. Take a snapshot first so there is a state to certify.</p></details>
			<div id="tamperResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<p class="hint"><button type="button" id="tamperLogBtn" class="link-btn">Show tamper history</button> · <button type="button" id="tamperAttestList" class="link-btn">Show timestamp proofs</button></p>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button type="button" id="tamperAttest" class="ghost-btn">Timestamp proof</button>
				<button type="button" id="tamperSnapshot" class="ghost-btn">Take snapshot</button>
				<button type="button" id="tamperSeal" class="ghost-btn">Seal</button>
				<button value="ok" id="tamperAudit" class="primary-btn">Audit now</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="protectDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="protectForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Self-healing</h3>
			<p id="protectName" class="muted"></p>
			<p class="hint">Adds recovery data inside the vault so random corruption or bit‑rot can be repaired — useful for a vault kept on an aging drive or in a synced folder. It works over the encrypted files, so it needs no password and never exposes your contents. It is <strong>not a backup</strong>: it can't help with a deleted, lost, or ransomware‑hit vault, so keep an off‑site copy too.</p>
			<p id="protectStatus" class="hint"></p>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Recovery strength</span>
				<select id="protectTier">
					<option value="low">Low — about 5% extra space</option>
					<option value="medium" selected>Medium — about 10% extra space (recommended)</option>
					<option value="high">High — about 15% extra space</option>
				</select>
				<small class="checkhint">Higher strength repairs more damage but stores more recovery data. It refreshes on its own after you change the vault's contents.</small>
			</label>
			<label class="checkfield">
				<input type="checkbox" id="protectThorough">
				<span>Thorough change detection</span>
			</label>
			<details class="advanced"><summary>When to turn this on</summary><p class="mounthint">Also refreshes the recovery data after an edit that keeps a file the same size — for a vault holding a database or a disk image rewritten in place. It re-reads the vault on each unmount, so leave it off for ordinary files.</p></details>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Scheduled check</span>
				<select id="scrubMode">
					<option value="off">Off</option>
					<option value="daily">Daily</option>
					<option value="weekly">Weekly</option>
				</select>
			</label>
			<label class="checkfield" id="scrubHealRow" hidden>
				<input type="checkbox" id="scrubHeal">
				<span>Auto-repair any damage found</span>
			</label>
			<p class="mounthint">While the vault sits idle and unmounted, periodically checks its recovery data for bit-rot — no password needed. Turn on Auto-repair to fix any damage automatically from the recovery data. Runs while the web interface is open.</p>
			<div class="field" id="scrubNowRow" hidden>
				<button type="button" id="scrubNow" class="link-btn">Check now</button>
				<span class="mounthint" id="scrubNowResult"></span>
			</div>
			<div id="protectProgress" class="progress" hidden>
				<div class="progress-track"><div id="protectBarFill" class="progress-fill"></div></div>
				<div id="protectBarText" class="progress-text"></div>
			</div>
			<div id="protectResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button type="button" id="protectRemove" class="link-btn danger" hidden>Remove</button>
				<button type="button" id="protectHeal" class="ghost-btn" hidden>Check &amp; repair</button>
				<button type="button" id="protectGo" class="primary-btn">Add / update protection</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="mirrorDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="mirrorForm">
			<h3>Mirror</h3>
			<p id="mirrorName" class="muted"></p>
			<p class="hint">Keep a two‑way copy of this vault on another drive, a network share, or an SFTP server. Only the encrypted files travel — the destination never sees your password or your contents. It is <strong>not a backup</strong>: a mirror also copies deletions, so keep a separate backup for that.</p>
			<details class="advanced"><summary>How syncing works</summary><p class="hint">It syncs both directions when you click Sync, and on its own after you unmount the vault. Setting up a mirror first primes it — the destination is made to match this vault, and it never overwrites an unrelated folder or a different vault.</p></details>
			<p id="mirrorStatus" class="hint"></p>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Destination</span>
				<select id="mirrorDest"></select>
			</label>
			<div id="mirrorResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button type="button" id="mirrorRemove" class="link-btn danger" hidden>Stop mirroring</button>
				<button type="button" id="mirrorSync" class="ghost-btn" hidden>Sync now</button>
				<button type="button" id="mirrorGo" class="primary-btn">Set up mirror</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="peerDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="peerForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Peer node</h3>
			<p class="hint">A peer is another machine running <strong>Serve</strong> for this vault. Easiest: paste the one-line <strong>connect code</strong> it showed. Only encrypted files ever cross the connection.</p>
			<label class="field"><span>Connect code <span class="muted">(paste — carries everything)</span></span><input type="text" class="mask" id="peerCode" placeholder="paste the code from the other machine" autocomplete="off" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true"></label>
			<details class="advanced"><summary>Or enter the details by hand</summary>
			<label class="field"><span>Label</span><input type="text" id="peerLabel" placeholder="Home desktop" autocomplete="off"></label>
			<label class="field"><span>Address</span><input type="text" id="peerUrl" placeholder="http://127.0.0.1:8790/  or  https://tunnel.example.com/" autocomplete="off"></label>
			<div class="row">
				<label class="field"><span>Username</span><input type="text" id="peerUser" value="vd" autocomplete="off"></label>
				<label class="field"><span>Password</span><input type="text" class="mask" id="peerPass" autocomplete="off" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true"></label>
			</div>
			</details>
			<div id="peerResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Cancel</button>
				<button type="button" id="peerTest" class="ghost-btn">Test</button>
				<button type="button" id="peerSave" class="primary-btn">Save peer</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="serveDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="serveForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Serve as a node</h3>
			<p id="serveName" class="muted"></p>
			<p class="hint">Make this vault reachable from another machine so it can mirror to yours. Only the encrypted files are served — your password and contents never leave this computer. The vault must be unmounted while it is served.</p>
			<label class="field" id="serveBindRow">
				<span>Reachable on</span>
				<select id="serveBind">
					<option value="relay" selected>Anywhere, through a relay hub — no port forwarding (easiest)</option>
					<option value="127.0.0.1">This machine only — reach it through your own tunnel or VPN</option>
					<option value="0.0.0.0">This whole local network (LAN)</option>
				</select>
				<small class="checkhint">The relay reaches this node from any network with no port forwarding — you run a small hub on any machine with a public address (a cheap VPS) and paste its details once below. “This machine only” is safest if you already run a tunnel/VPN. “LAN” exposes it to your local network, protected by the generated password.</small>
			</label>
			<div id="serveRelayRow" class="field">
				<div class="row">
					<label class="field"><span>Relay hub address</span><input type="text" id="serveRelayHost" placeholder="hub.example.com:7443" autocomplete="off"></label>
					<label class="field"><span>Relay token</span><input type="text" class="mask" id="serveRelayToken" autocomplete="off" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true"></label>
				</div>
				<small class="checkhint">Run the hub with <code>vdisk relay</code> on a machine with a public address; it prints the address and token to paste here. Only encrypted files cross the relay.</small>
			</div>
			<div id="serveConn" class="conn" hidden>
				<div class="conn-row" id="serveCodeRow" hidden><span class="conn-label">Connect code</span><code id="serveCode" class="conn-val"></code><button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy="#serveCode" aria-label="Copy connect code">Copy</button></div>
				<div class="conn-row"><span class="conn-label">Address</span><code id="serveUrl" class="conn-val"></code><button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy="#serveUrl" aria-label="Copy address">Copy</button></div>
				<div class="conn-row"><span class="conn-label">Username</span><code id="serveUser" class="conn-val"></code><button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy="#serveUser" aria-label="Copy username">Copy</button></div>
				<div class="conn-row"><span class="conn-label">Password</span><code id="servePass" class="conn-val"></code><button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy="#servePass" aria-label="Copy password">Copy</button></div>
				<p class="hint" id="serveConnHint">On the other machine, open <strong>Mirror → Add a peer node…</strong> and paste the <strong>Connect code</strong> (it carries everything). If you served “this machine only”, point your tunnel/VPN at the address and use that there instead.</p>
				<p class="hint" id="serveSecNote"></p>
			</div>
			<div id="serveResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button type="button" id="serveStop" class="link-btn danger" hidden>Stop serving</button>
				<button type="button" id="serveGo" class="primary-btn">Start serving</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<!-- Tier 3: split a vault across places -->
	<dialog id="disperseDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="disperseForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Split across places</h3>
			<p id="disperseName" class="muted"></p>
			<p class="hint">Splits this vault into pieces spread across several folders — external drives, network shares, or folders on other machines. Any few of them rebuild it, and no single piece reveals anything. This is for durability, not daily use: to open the vault again you rebuild it first. It is <strong>not a backup substitute</strong> for a vault you use every day.</p>
			<label class="field"><span>Destination folders — one shard each</span></label>
			<div id="disperseDests" class="dest-list"></div>
			<button type="button" id="disperseAddDest" class="ghost-btn">Add a folder…</button>
			<label class="field mt-3">
				<span>Rebuild threshold — how many shards are needed to rebuild</span>
				<input type="number" id="disperseK" min="1" value="2" step="1">
				<small class="checkhint" id="disperseGuidance"></small>
			</label>
			<div id="disperseProgress" class="progress" hidden>
				<div class="progress-track"><div id="disperseBarFill" class="progress-fill"></div></div>
				<div id="disperseBarText" class="progress-text"></div>
			</div>
			<div id="disperseResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button type="button" id="disperseGo" class="primary-btn">Split</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<!-- Tier 3: rebuild a vault from its shards -->
	<dialog id="rebuildDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="rebuildForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Rebuild from shards</h3>
			<p class="hint">Point at the folders that hold the shards (the ones you split the vault into). Enough of them together rebuild the whole vault. Open it afterwards with your password as usual.</p>
			<label class="field"><span>Folders holding the shards</span></label>
			<div id="rebuildFolders" class="dest-list"></div>
			<button type="button" id="rebuildAddFolder" class="ghost-btn">Add a folder…</button>
			<div class="dialog-actions dialog-actions-inline actions-left">
				<button type="button" id="rebuildCheck" class="ghost-btn">Check shards</button>
				<button type="button" id="rebuildRepair" class="ghost-btn" hidden>Repair missing shards</button>
			</div>
			<div id="rebuildStatus" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<label class="field mt-1">
				<span>Keep these folders healthy automatically</span>
				<select id="rebuildSchedule">
					<option value="off">Off</option>
					<option value="interval:12">Every 12 hours</option>
					<option value="interval:24">Every day</option>
					<option value="interval:168">Every week</option>
				</select>
				<small class="checkhint">Checks these shard folders on this schedule and re-creates any missing or damaged shards — while the web interface is running.</small>
			</label>
			<label class="field mt-1">
				<span>Rebuild into this folder</span>
				<div class="row">
					<input type="text" id="rebuildDest" class="grow" placeholder="Click Browse to choose a folder, or paste its path">
					<button type="button" id="rebuildDestBrowse" class="ghost-btn">Browse…</button>
				</div>
			</label>
			<div id="rebuildProgress" class="progress" hidden>
				<div class="progress-track"><div id="rebuildBarFill" class="progress-fill"></div></div>
				<div id="rebuildBarText" class="progress-text"></div>
			</div>
			<div id="rebuildResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button type="button" id="rebuildGo" class="primary-btn">Rebuild</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<!-- Tier 3: threshold-split the unlock key -->
	<dialog id="thresholdDialog" class="dialog">
		<form method="dialog" id="thresholdForm" autocomplete="off">
			<h3>Threshold key</h3>
			<p id="thresholdName" class="muted"></p>
			<p class="hint">Splits the vault's unlock key into shares, so no single person or place can open it. You choose how many shares to make and how many are needed together to unlock. Give one share to each holder. The full key is never written to disk — it is reassembled in memory only at the moment of unlocking.</p>
			<div class="row">
				<label class="field"><span>Total shares</span><input type="number" id="thresholdN" min="2" value="3" step="1"></label>
				<label class="field"><span>Needed to unlock</span><input type="number" id="thresholdK" min="2" value="2" step="1"></label>
			</div>
			<label class="field">
				<span>Current password</span>
				<input type="text" class="mask" autocomplete="off" data-1p-ignore data-lpignore="true" id="thresholdPass">
			</label>
			<label class="checkfield"><input type="checkbox" id="thresholdReadOnly"> <span>Read-only (for emergency / inheritance access) — the holders can open and read the vault, but can never change it or lock you out. Recommended when the shares go to trusted contacts.</span></label>
			<div id="thresholdShares" class="conn" hidden></div>
			<div id="thresholdResult" class="tamper-result" hidden></div>
			<div class="dialog-actions">
				<button type="button" value="cancel" class="ghost-btn">Close</button>
				<button type="button" id="thresholdGo" class="primary-btn">Create shares</button>
			</div>
		</form>
	</dialog>

	<div id="toast" class="toast" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" popover="manual" hidden></div>

	<script src="/js/qrcode.js"></script>
	<script src="/js/strength.js"></script>
	<script src="/js/webauthn.js"></script>
	<script src="/js/vendor/marked/marked.min.js"></script>
	<script src="/shared/secret-schema.js"></script>
	<script src="/js/app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
